  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "proctoscope_section_1",
      "section_title": "Proctoscope",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_1",
              "group_title": "Proctoscope (Proctoscope / Anoscope distinction)",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A proctoscope is an instrument used to examine the anal canal and rectum, particularly the distal rectum, by allowing direct visualisation of the mucosa."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is used in general surgery, colorectal surgery and gastroenterology to investigate symptoms such as rectal bleeding, anal discomfort and suspected distal rectal lesions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key OSCE identification point: A short, rigid, hollow tube with an open viewing end; many models have an obturator to facilitate insertion and a light source or attachment for illumination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Terminology varies between institutions and manufacturers. An anoscope is designed primarily for the anal canal, whereas a proctoscope generally allows examination of a greater length of the distal rectum."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Classification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Specialty: General and colorectal surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Function: Diagnostic examination instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Design: Rigid or, in some systems, flexible."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Material: Stainless steel or medical-grade plastic."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Viewing method: Direct vision, often with illumination."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Common accessories: Obturator, light source and, in selected designs, an attachment for suction or instrumentation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rigid instruments are common in basic surgical OSCE stations."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Barrel or tube",
                      "Maintains an open passage for visualisation of the anal canal and distal rectum."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Distal tip",
                      "The inserted end; should be smooth and atraumatic."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Obturator",
                      "Temporarily fills the barrel during insertion, helping provide a smoother leading surface."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Handle or grip",
                      "Allows controlled manipulation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Viewing aperture",
                      "Provides access for visual inspection and, in suitable models, instruments."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Light source or light attachment",
                      "Illuminates the mucosa for examination."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not every model has all these components. Some instruments have integrated illumination, while others use a separate light source."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Types of proctoscope",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Rigid proctoscope"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Straight, rigid tube."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly used for direct examination of the anal canal and distal rectum."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Often has a removable obturator and a separate or attached illumination system."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suitable for selected biopsies and procedures using compatible instruments."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. Flexible endoscope for distal bowel examination"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Flexible insertion tube with a camera and light source."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can examine farther into the sigmoid colon when used as a flexible sigmoidoscope."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allows image display and targeted biopsy with compatible equipment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "More complex, expensive and dependent on appropriate endoscopy facilities."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam clarification: A flexible sigmoidoscope is not simply another name for every proctoscope. It is designed to examine the rectum and sigmoid colon, usually beyond the reach of a standard rigid proctoscope."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Indications and clinical uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Indication",
                    "What the instrument helps assess"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Rectal bleeding",
                      "Haemorrhoids, mucosal lesions, ulcers and distal rectal masses."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Suspected rectal polyp",
                      "Location and appearance; biopsy may be possible."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Rectal pain or discharge",
                      "Selected mucosal and distal rectal abnormalities."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Suspected proctitis",
                      "Inflammation, erythema, friability or ulceration."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Suspected distal rectal tumour",
                      "Visible lesion requiring appropriate diagnostic investigation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Foreign body assessment",
                      "Selected cases, depending on clinical stability and the location of the object."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Follow-up examination",
                      "Selected known distal rectal conditions."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Proctoscopy can help identify the source of bleeding, but finding haemorrhoids does not automatically exclude colorectal cancer or another proximal source. Further evaluation may be necessary depending on the patient's age, symptoms, examination and risk factors."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides direct visualisation of the anal canal and distal rectum."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can be performed relatively quickly in an appropriate clinical setting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Often does not require sedation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May permit targeted biopsy using compatible instruments."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires less extensive preparation than a full colonoscopy in many cases."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can help identify the cause of selected anorectal symptoms."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examines a limited length of bowel compared with flexible sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May cause discomfort, particularly with anal disease or poor technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires adequate lighting and appropriate patient positioning."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stool or blood may obscure the mucosa."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A rigid instrument is less adaptable to the bowel's curves."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Findings may necessitate further endoscopy, imaging or histological assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The procedure should be deferred or specialist assessment obtained when the risks outweigh the benefits."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important contraindications or situations requiring particular caution include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suspected acute bowel perforation or peritonitis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Haemodynamic instability or shock."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe anal pain or marked anal stenosis that makes safe insertion difficult."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Recent anorectal surgery, depending on the operation and healing status."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe acute anorectal inflammation or suspected abscess."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Poor patient cooperation when safe examination cannot be achieved."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact contraindications depend on the clinical situation and whether the proposed examination is a simple proctoscopy or a more extensive endoscopic procedure."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "How to perform proctoscopy in an OSCE",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Step 1 — Preparation"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Introduce yourself, confirm the patient's identity, explain the examination and obtain consent. Ensure privacy, use a chaperone according to local policy, perform hand hygiene and prepare gloves, lubricant, light source and proctoscope."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Step 2 — Positioning"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The left lateral position is commonly used, with hips and knees flexed. Other positions, including the knee-chest position, may be used depending on the setting and clinical purpose."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Step 3 — Preliminary examination"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the perianal area and perform a digital rectal examination when appropriate, assessing tenderness, masses, sphincter tone and palpable abnormalities. Do not proceed routinely if significant pain or suspected injury makes insertion unsafe."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Step 4 — Insertion"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lubricate the instrument and insert it gently according to the manufacturer's design and standard clinical technique. The obturator, where present, facilitates insertion and is removed once the instrument is appropriately positioned."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Step 5 — Visual inspection"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Illuminate the lumen and inspect the mucosa systematically. Withdraw slowly while maintaining the correct orientation, noting haemorrhoids, ulcers, inflammation, polyps, masses and bleeding. Obtain a biopsy only when indicated and using suitable equipment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/proctoscope_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Step 6 — Completion"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Withdraw carefully, ensure patient comfort, document findings and arrange further investigation if required. Dispose of single-use equipment or reprocess reusable instruments according to the manufacturer's instructions and infection-prevention policy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These steps describe the general examination sequence. Actual insertion technique and preparation should follow supervised clinical training and the specific instrument design."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Normal and abnormal findings",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Finding",
                    "Possible interpretation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Smooth, intact mucosa",
                      "Normal appearance, depending on the area examined."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Dilated vascular cushions",
                      "Haemorrhoidal disease, interpreted with symptoms and examination."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Erythematous, friable mucosa",
                      "Proctitis or another inflammatory process."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ulceration",
                      "Inflammatory, infectious, traumatic or neoplastic causes."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Polypoid lesion",
                      "Rectal polyp or another mucosal lesion; histology may be required."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Irregular mass",
                      "Suspicious for neoplasia; requires appropriate investigation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fresh blood",
                      "Active or recent bleeding; source must be assessed."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Faecal material obscuring the view",
                      "Inadequate visualisation; examination may be limited."
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Prevention"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Discomfort or pain",
                      "Explain the procedure and use gentle technique."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Minor rectal bleeding",
                      "Avoid force and unnecessary manipulation."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mucosal trauma",
                      "Use adequate lubrication and an appropriate instrument size."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection transmission",
                      "Follow appropriate decontamination and sterilisation protocols."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Rare rectal perforation",
                      "Avoid forceful insertion, particularly in vulnerable tissue or difficult anatomy."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Missed pathology",
                      "Recognise the limits of the examination and arrange further evaluation when indicated."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Serious complications are uncommon, but suspected perforation requires urgent assessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify this instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A proctoscope, used to examine the anal canal and distal rectum."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the function of the obturator?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It fills the barrel during insertion and provides a smoother leading surface to facilitate atraumatic introduction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the difference between a proctoscope and an anoscope?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An anoscope is primarily designed to examine the anal canal, while a proctoscope generally permits examination of the distal rectum as well."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the difference between proctoscopy and sigmoidoscopy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Proctoscopy focuses on the anal canal and distal rectum. Sigmoidoscopy examines the rectum and sigmoid colon, using a rigid or flexible instrument depending on the procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Name four indications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rectal bleeding, suspected rectal lesions, selected cases of proctitis, and assessment of suspected distal rectal polyps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Why perform a digital rectal examination first?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "When appropriate, it helps assess tenderness, masses, sphincter tone and palpable abnormalities before instrument insertion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Does finding haemorrhoids exclude rectal cancer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Persistent bleeding, anaemia, weight loss, altered bowel habits or other concerning features may require further investigation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. State two important complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rectal bleeding and mucosal injury; perforation is rare but potentially serious."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "proctoscope_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "Examiner's checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "proctoscope_group_14",
